Defensive & Forensic Drone/UAV Communications Security
Current Research Baseline for a Bachelor’s Cybersecurity Capstone
Research window emphasized: 2025 through 9 September 2026

Purpose
This brief maps recent academic, vulnerability, laboratory, practitioner, and government work relevant to an integrated defensive and forensic UAV communications platform. It emphasizes passive RF discovery, packet/protocol analysis, authorized-lab threat modeling, cryptographic implementation posture, evidence preservation, and lawful counter-UAS detection/attribution use cases.
Current team hardware: VTI Phoenix 2.0 and TTROARDS TT19. Open-ecosystem expansion targets include MAVLink, ArduPilot, PX4, and Pixhawk.
Safety and legal scope: Testing should remain confined to owned/authorized systems and controlled RF environments. Counter-UAS mitigation authority is jurisdiction- and actor-specific; the most transferable capstone value is in passive detection, attribution, evidence, protocol hardening, and instrumented testbed research.

Validation legend
•	Simulation — software-only modeling or synthetic evaluation.
•	Software-in-the-Loop (SITL) — autopilot/protocol software executed without a physical flight controller.
•	Hardware-in-the-Loop (HIL) — real hardware integrated with simulated or partially simulated flight environment.
•	Physical UAV — real flight hardware or a real drone testbed is used.
•	Commercial/Proprietary UAV — consumer/enterprise product or proprietary ecosystem is directly tested.

Executive summary
The strongest 2026 evidence reinforces a cross-layer capstone direction rather than a single exploit or classifier. Recent RF work can passively identify drones and classify operating behavior; recent MAVLink work demonstrates signing/timestamp weaknesses and stealthy relay behavior; recent forensics work improves deleted-log recovery and tool validation; and airport guidance increasingly stresses multi-sensor detection, interoperability, evidence, spectrum coordination, and stakeholder workflow.
Two August 2026 DJI CVEs are especially important for the cryptography workstream because they show how an otherwise strong encrypted Wi-Fi link can be undermined by adjacent trust failures: BLE transport exposes Wi-Fi credentials and trusted identifiers in cleartext in one case, while an unauthenticated BLE DUML interface can modify wireless configuration in another. This is exactly the distinction the capstone should make between attacking AES itself and attacking key handling, authentication, trust tokens, replay assumptions, and protocol composition.
The open UAV ecosystem remains a valuable controlled research target. CVE-2026-1579 documents the consequences of MAVLink interfaces without message signing, while a 2026 IEEE Transactions on Communications paper demonstrates a timestamp/GPS manipulation attack against MAVLink 2.0 signing without knowing the secret key, validated across simulation, HIL, and a hardware environment. Those results support a detector that correlates protocol signing state, timestamps, navigation/time sources, and command-flow anomalies instead of relying on payload signatures alone.
Three 2026 preprints further sharpen the boundary of prior art: one evaluates secure RF/Wi-Fi communication across a four-UAV physical swarm testbed, one compiles verified MAVLink runtime monitors to allocation-free C and evaluates them in ArduPilot SITL, and one integrates RF/acoustic/vision sensing on public datasets. They broaden crypto, protocol-monitoring, and sensor-fusion evidence, but still do not join RF discovery, packet interpretation, cross-ecosystem protocol evidence, and forensic reconstruction in one reusable platform.
A defensible novelty claim is not that RF classification, packet capture, protocol reverse engineering, intrusion detection, cryptographic hardening, or drone forensics are individually new. The gap is integration. In the recent sources reviewed here, these capabilities are usually isolated by layer or ecosystem. A capstone can contribute an extensible “UAV Communications Observatory & Forensic Testbed” that passively identifies communication technologies, records packet and RF evidence, decodes open protocols, maps bidirectional flows, correlates telemetry/log artifacts, scores security posture, and validates controlled threat detections across both proprietary consumer drones and MAVLink systems.

