Immediate hardware-specific opportunities

VTI Phoenix 2.0
The available Phoenix 2.0 manual identifies a 2.440–2.471 GHz remote-control link and a 5 GHz Wi-Fi image-transmission system, with the phone connecting to an SSID in the form “Phoenix Drone ****.” This gives the team a clean two-link starting point: characterize the controller RF separately from the app/video Wi-Fi link, then correlate observed spectrum occupancy with packet-level traffic during mode changes. [33]
•	Passive RF baseline: controller off/on, drone off/on, pairing, idle, motors armed, takeoff, video idle/active, app-only control, Return-to-Home, and GPS-assisted modes.
•	Packet baseline: identify DHCP/DNS/local service discovery, transport protocols, traffic direction, video flow, control messages, and whether telemetry/GPS appears on the Wi-Fi side.
•	Evidence goal: synchronized SDR timestamps + PCAP + screen/app event log + onboard/media timestamps, enabling cross-layer event reconstruction.

TTROARDS TT19
Public TT19 documentation consistently indicates 2.4 GHz remote control plus a phone Wi-Fi/FPV connection, although SSID naming and exact app details vary by product revision. That ambiguity is useful research rather than a blocker: the team can make hardware identification and communications discovery an explicit first-stage capability instead of assuming published marketing specifications are authoritative. [34]
•	Determine whether RC and FPV/app links occupy the same 2.4 GHz band and measure channel behavior, coexistence, and mode-dependent activity.
•	Map the phone-drone LAN and identify ports, flows, frame rates, directionality, and any commands sent from the app interface.
•	Preserve a revision fingerprint: controller FCC markings, app package/version, SSID/BSSID, MAC OUIs, channel width, observed modulation characteristics, and firmware/app metadata where legally obtainable.

Gap analysis and capstone opportunity
Based on the reviewed 2025–2026 sources, no single recent platform was identified that integrates passive RF technology discovery, packet/protocol interpretation, controlled threat detection, cryptographic posture analysis, and forensic evidence correlation across both proprietary and open UAV ecosystems. This is an analytical gap statement, not a claim that no such system exists anywhere; it is the most defensible novelty position supported by the current source set.
Proposed integrated platform: UAV Communications Observatory & Forensic Testbed
1. RF discovery and fingerprinting — SDR-based spectral survey; detect likely UAV/controller activity; identify center frequencies/bandwidth; classify signal families; optionally perform device fingerprinting when sufficient training data exists.
2. Packet and flow capture — Capture Wi-Fi/UDP/TCP/MAVLink where accessible; retain PCAP/PCAPNG; classify GCS→UAV vs UAV→GCS; visualize session timing, rates, ports, message types, and state transitions.
3. Protocol adapters — Native MAVLink decoder first; plugin interface for proprietary/partially reversed protocols; map telemetry, GPS, battery, mode, commands, acknowledgements, and video/control channels without assuming one vendor.
4. Authorized threat emulator — In SITL/HIL and owned lab hardware, reproduce benign relay, replay, malformed-message, timestamp, and configuration-change test cases needed to validate detection. Avoid field deployment of takeover/jamming functionality.
5. Cryptographic posture analyzer — Record whether message signing is enabled; flag cleartext credential/trust material, static/reused secrets where observable, replay/timestamp anomalies, unauthenticated control paths, weak session binding, and protocol downgrade/version conditions. Evaluate AES implementations as systems, not as brute-force targets.
6. Forensic evidence layer — Hash captures/logs; synchronize clocks; preserve provenance; correlate SDR events, PCAPs, app/controller logs, flight-controller logs, GPS/telemetry, and analyst annotations; produce reproducible incident timelines.
7. Airport/enterprise mode — Passive detection/attribution profile with no mitigation action: RF observations, confidence, track/event correlation, device identity evidence, standardized export, and integration hooks for other DTI sensors or SOC/SIEM workflows.

