Cryptography: what to test instead of “breaking AES”
AES-128 and AES-256 are not realistic brute-force targets for this capstone. The experimentally useful question is whether the surrounding system preserves the assumptions that make AES secure. Recent UAV evidence supports the following attack-surface taxonomy:
•	Key exposure and transport — e.g., credentials or trust identifiers carried in cleartext on an adjacent interface, as in CVE-2026-77812.
•	Missing authentication — encrypted or local wireless connectivity does not help if a control interface accepts unauthenticated configuration/command messages, as illustrated by CVE-2026-78306 and unsigned MAVLink.
•	Replay/timestamp/session binding — signed protocols can still fail if freshness or time-source assumptions can be manipulated; the 2026 MAVLink timestamp study is directly relevant.
•	Key derivation/storage and session lifecycle — compare static PSKs, per-session keys, hardware-bound keys, PUF-derived keys, and authenticated encryption designs; document how key material is provisioned and rotated.
•	Nonce/IV uniqueness and cipher mode — instrument owned systems or test implementations to detect repeat/non-unique nonces/IVs, misuse of unauthenticated encryption, and cipher/version downgrade opportunities.
•	Endpoint compromise and app/controller trust — treat the phone app, controller, companion computer, firmware update path, and local services as part of the cryptographic system boundary.
For a sponsor-facing demo, the strongest contrast is: “AES remains cryptographically strong; our platform verifies whether the surrounding UAV protocol actually uses keys, authentication, freshness, and session state safely.”
Airport, private-contractor, and technology-transfer relevance
Airport-focused sponsorship is most credible if the capstone emphasizes detection, attribution, evidence, interoperability, and test/validation rather than independent mitigation. FAA airport guidance calls out RF, radar, EO, and acoustic detection, warns about electromagnetic and operational impacts, and requires coordination for on-airport systems. The JRC C-UAS report similarly frames DTI as detection, localisation, tracking, classification, and identification, with sensor fusion as a central operational issue. [21–23]
The U.S. legal landscape changed materially in 2026: DOJ/DHS issued an interim final rule implementing the SAFER SKIES Act framework for certain State, local, Tribal, and territorial law-enforcement/correctional agencies, including separate training/certification structures for detection/warning and mitigation, plus authorized-technology, spectrum, airspace, ATC-notification, reporting, privacy, and compliance requirements. Private contractors do not simply inherit independent mitigation authority; September 2026 Professional Services Council comments specifically ask DOJ/DHS to clarify contractor technical/operational support and developmental testing. [24, 26]
A sponsor-friendly transfer path is therefore a passive or non-interfering “communications observability” platform: RF sensor integration, protocol-aware evidence, confidence scoring, event timelines, standards-aligned exports, and reproducible test methodology. EUROCAE WG-115 is actively developing airport C-UAS operational and interoperability standards, including ED-286A targeted for publication in December 2026, which makes interface design and performance metrics particularly timely. [25]

