The matrix shows the core novelty opportunity: most recent work is deep in one or two layers. The capstone becomes materially different when the same timestamped case file can connect “RF energy appeared” → “a specific network/protocol flow began” → “a command or anomaly occurred” → “onboard/controller logs recorded the result” → “the evidence can be reproduced and validated.”

Measurable outcomes for a sponsor-facing capstone

RF discovery — Detection probability / false-alarm rate; center-frequency and bandwidth estimation error; device/model classification accuracy; time-to-identification.

Packet/protocol — Percent of captured traffic automatically classified; percent of MAVLink message types decoded; GCS-to-UAV / UAV-to-GCS flow attribution accuracy; telemetry/GPS extraction accuracy.

Threat detection — Detection rate, false-positive rate, detection latency, and disagreement rate across rule/signature vs statistical/ML detectors.

Cryptographic posture — Correct identification of signing/authentication status; replay/timestamp anomaly detection; static/reused credential/session findings in owned test systems; measured crypto overhead where applicable.

Forensics — Cross-source timestamp alignment error; recovered artifact completeness; hash/provenance verification; event-reconstruction accuracy against a scripted ground-truth flight.

Portability — Number of supported ecosystems/models; time required to add a new protocol/RF profile; percentage of core pipeline reused across Phoenix 2.0, TT19, and MAVLink/Pixhawk targets.

Operational fit — CPU/memory/storage cost at sensor/edge node; analyst time to produce an incident report; standardized export/API completeness; passive-sensor non-interference verification.

Recommended next research/design decisions
•	Make “cross-layer correlation” the thesis of the platform, not “we hacked a drone.” The literature already contains many isolated attacks; integration, reproducibility, and evidence quality are more defensible contributions.
•	Use MAVLink/PX4/ArduPilot as the fully instrumentable reference ecosystem and Phoenix 2.0/TT19 as proprietary black-box comparison targets. This prevents the project from depending on successful reverse engineering of one low-cost consumer model.
•	Treat RF discovery as Stage 0. A system that first determines which band/link is active before selecting capture/decoder modules is more adaptable and more relevant to airport detection workflows.
•	Build a strict evidence model early: monotonic + wall-clock timestamps, capture hashes, device/app/firmware metadata, test-case IDs, operator actions, and ground-truth labels. Apply the 2026 drone-forensic tool-testing methodology to your own outputs.
•	For cryptography, demonstrate secure-vs-insecure system composition: signed vs unsigned MAVLink, freshness/time anomalies, static vs rotated keys in your own test implementation, and detection of credential/trust leakage conditions. Do not make brute-force AES a milestone.
•	Keep airport “mitigation” as a policy/testbed integration topic unless the team has explicit institutional authorization. Demonstrate safe-land/shutdown logic in SITL/HIL or through legitimate APIs on owned equipment, and focus physical RF work on passive sensing and evidence.

